From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: James Gardner <jamestexasgardner@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 12:00:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] vmm: a guest that records no exit status does not exit 0

The guest init records the workload's exit status, or its own refusal,
through the root virtio-fs device (KRUN_EXIT_CODE_IOCTL). When the VM
stops and nothing was recorded, the VMM fell back to the vcpu's code,
which is "OK" whenever the guest merely restarted: after a kernel
panic (panic=-1), after init died, or after a workload run as PID 1
(KRUN_INIT_PID1) exited, whatever its status. The process then exited
0, and an embedder read a run that never completed as one that
succeeded.

When the guest recorded no status and the vcpu reports "OK", the VMM
now exits NO_GUEST_EXIT_STATUS (125, the code init uses for a failure
of the runtime itself) and says so on stderr. A status the guest
recorded, including 0, still wins, and a vcpu error code is still
reported as it was.

Signed-off-by: James Gardner <jamestexasgardner@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/src/vmm/src/lib.rs b/src/vmm/src/lib.rs
--- a/src/vmm/src/lib.rs
+++ b/src/vmm/src/lib.rs
@@ -67,6 +67,11 @@

 /// Success exit code.
 pub const FC_EXIT_CODE_OK: u8 = 0;
+/// The process exit status when the guest stopped without recording one
+/// (its init records the workload's status, or its own refusal, through the
+/// root virtio-fs device). Init's code for a failure of the runtime itself,
+/// as podman and crun use it.
+pub const NO_GUEST_EXIT_STATUS: i32 = 125;
 /// Generic error exit code.
 pub const FC_EXIT_CODE_GENERIC_ERROR: u8 = 1;
 /// Generic exit code for an error considered not possible to occur if the program logic is sound.
@@ -423,6 +428,16 @@
             let exit_code = if vmm_exit_code != i32::MAX {
                 debug!("using vmm exit code: {vmm_exit_code}");
                 vmm_exit_code
+            } else if vcpu_exit_code == FC_EXIT_CODE_OK {
+                // The guest recorded no exit status: init died, the kernel
+                // panicked, or the VM stopped before the workload's status
+                // was recorded. An "OK" from the vcpu here only means the
+                // guest restarted, so it is not reported as success.
+                eprintln!(
+                    "libkrun: the guest stopped without recording an exit status; \
+                     exiting {NO_GUEST_EXIT_STATUS}"
+                );
+                NO_GUEST_EXIT_STATUS
             } else {
                 debug!("using vcpu exit code: {vcpu_exit_code}");
                 vcpu_exit_code as i32
